Exit Criteria
Pass 9 is complete only when:
Real nodes communicate across supported platforms.
Distributed workloads execute successfully.
Failover and recovery are validated.
Release artifacts remain reproducible.
All production blockers are documented.
The repository is ready to begin Release Candidate stabilization (Pass 10).

Summary
Restored package-level ZDXNode compatibility with a portable coordinator client supporting identity registration, heartbeats, frame hashing, and frame announcements. 

Added a thread-safe, deterministic, capability-aware scheduler with node removal and failover behavior. 

Hardened protocol decoding to reject malformed envelopes, invalid field types, incompatible versions, and oversized messages before dispatch. 

Added TLS 1.2+ client and server context factories, including optional mandatory mutual TLS when a client CA is configured. 

Improved coordinator reliability by isolating malformed clients, accepting optional TLS, and closing the listening socket during graceful shutdown. 

Added regression tests covering protocol incompatibility, malformed and oversized packets, capability negotiation, and scheduler failover. 

Corrected the authentication integration test callback so its argument order matches the documented authentication pipeline API. 

Synchronized the README, changelog, roadmap, security model, protocol documentation, test plan, release notes, development status, and authoritative TODO. The documentation explicitly states that Pass 9 remains in progress rather than claiming unsupported Release Candidate readiness. 

Documented the security findings: workload authorization is not yet bound to mTLS identity, rotation remains manual, raw TCP rate limiting and coordinator consensus are absent, persistence is not transactional or encrypted, and independent fuzzing and penetration testing remain required. 

Documented potential commercial opportunities—including managed coordinators, fleet observability, certificate lifecycle management, audit exports, support, and verified workload marketplaces—along with the prerequisite privacy, isolation, billing, SLA, and licensing reviews. 

Committed the changes as 1234ded (Harden production network validation foundations) and created the requested pull request metadata.

Testing

✅ python -m pytest -q — 114 tests passed.

✅ python -m compileall -q .

✅ bash build.sh — native Cython extensions compiled and the distribution was assembled; Cython emitted a pre-existing advisory about negative indexing with wraparound=False.

✅ git diff --check

✅ git status --short --branch — clean working tree on branch work.

Remaining Production Blockers
Physical Linux/Windows/Android interoperability, multi-coordinator leader election, replicated persistent queues and leases, authenticated workload execution, certificate rotation, IPv6/NAT validation, observability, cross-platform reproducible builds, independent security testing, and a 24-hour soak test could not truthfully be validated inside this single Linux environment. They remain explicitly unchecked in the authoritative TODO. 


